=== layout: section

# Public, Environment, and Plant BioTech

=== layout: subsection

# 4. Purdue Pharma

=== layout: bookend

# 4. Questions

@body

- How did Purdue Pharma contribute specifically to the crisis?
- What does extended release change about oxycodone delivery?
- What tactics did Purdue Pharma use to promote OxyContin?

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. Extended Release and Risk Communication

@body

- OxyContin is an extended-release form of oxycodone. Extended release changes the timing of drug delivery.
- Opioid treatment can carry risks of dependence, addiction, and overdose.
- Purdue's marketing case asks what evidence prescribers and patients received about those risks.

@citation

[FDA, OxyContin prescribing information (2025)](https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/022272s052lbl.pdf)  |  [DOJ, Purdue Pharma guilty plea (2020)](https://www.justice.gov/usao-nj/pr/opioid-manufacturer-purdue-pharma-admits-guilt-fraud-and-kickback-conspiracies)

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. Separate a Product Claim from a Public Health Trend

@body

- Ask how a safety claim was tested and what population was studied.
- Purdue pleaded guilty in 2020 to federal charges related to its opioid practices.
- Marketing, prescribing, and changes in the illicit drug supply are distinct parts of the epidemic.

@citation

[DOJ, Purdue Pharma guilty plea (2020)](https://www.justice.gov/usao-nj/pr/opioid-manufacturer-purdue-pharma-admits-guilt-fraud-and-kickback-conspiracies)  |  [CDC, Understanding the opioid overdose epidemic](https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/understanding-the-opioid-overdose-epidemic.html)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Dopesick: A dramatization of the opioid crisis](assets/set7_instructor/dopesick.jpg)

@caption

Topic 4: Dopesick: A dramatization of the opioid crisis\
Compare what a drama portrays with what court records document.

@citation

Course image: instructor ethics deck, p. 42.

=== layout: two-panels

# 4. What Did Purdue Actually Admit?

@left

Richard Sackler, a former president of Purdue Pharma, is pictured giving a deposition. In 2020 the company pleaded guilty. It had continued marketing opioids to more than 100 prescribers it suspected of diverting them.

What could the plea establish about the company? What evidence would you need to attribute a death to its drug?

@right

![Richard Sackler during a deposition](assets/set7_people_early/richard_sackler.jpg)

@citation

[DOJ, Purdue plea (2020)](https://www.justice.gov/usao-nj/pr/opioid-manufacturer-purdue-pharma-admits-guilt-fraud-and-kickback-conspiracies)  |  [Deposition still: ProPublica](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Sackler_deposition_screenshot_%28cropped%29.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. One Trend, Several Drug Supplies

@body

The CDC describes overlapping waves of U.S. overdose deaths involving prescription opioids, then heroin, then illicitly manufactured fentanyl.

- A national total combines people exposed to different drugs.
- To evaluate a change in prescription marketing, compare the time period, place, and drugs involved.

@citation

[CDC, Understanding the opioid overdose epidemic](https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/understanding-the-opioid-overdose-epidemic.html)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![U.S. drug overdose deaths over time](assets/set7_environment_evidence/overdose_deaths.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 4: U.S. overdose deaths, 2014-2024\
Can this national count tell you how many deaths involved one company's drug?

@citation

[CDC NCHS, Drug overdose deaths in the United States, 2023-2024](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/products/databriefs/db549.htm)

=== layout: one-panel

# 4. What Does This Graph Answer?

@body

- The series rose from 70,630 deaths in 2019 to 107,941 in 2022.
- The 2024 count fell to 79,384, yet it remained above the 2019 count.
- The series includes every drug-overdose death. It cannot assign a death to Purdue or any specific supply.

@citation

[CDC NCHS, Drug overdose deaths in the United States, 2023-2024](https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/products/databriefs/db549.htm)  |  [DOJ, Purdue Pharma guilty plea (2020)](https://www.justice.gov/usao-nj/pr/opioid-manufacturer-purdue-pharma-admits-guilt-fraud-and-kickback-conspiracies)

=== layout: subsection

# 5. Andrew Wakefield and Public Harm

=== layout: bookend

# 5. Questions

@body

- Who is Andrew Wakefield?
- Why did his paper damage public trust?
- What evidence would you need to test the claim about MMR vaccination and autism?

=== layout: two-panels

# 5. What a Case Series Can Tell You

@left

- Andrew Wakefield and colleagues published a small case series in 1998. The paper was later retracted.
- Symptoms and vaccination can occur close together in time by chance.
- A small series with no comparison group cannot establish that vaccination caused autism.

@right

![Andrew Wakefield in 2010](assets/set7_people_early/andrew_wakefield.jpg)

@citation

[The Lancet, Retraction of the 1998 case series (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20137807/)  |  [Photo: MPR News (2010)](https://www.mprnews.org/story/2010/02/04/autism-study)

=== layout: two-panels

# 5. Who is missing from the picture?

@left

Children lined up for sugar cubes carrying polio vaccine at a Georgia school in 1961. The picture shows who came that day. Who would you look for outside the frame to study a vaccine's effects?

Now ask that question about MMR. Which two groups of children should the Danish study compare?

@right

![Children receive oral polio vaccine at a school in Decatur, Georgia, in 1961](assets/set7_people_early/polio_campaign_1961.jpg)

@citation

[Photo: CDC, Georgia polio campaign (1961)](https://wwwn.cdc.gov/phil/Details.aspx?pid=29084)  |  [Hviid et al., MMR cohort (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831578/)  |  [WHO review (2025)](https://www.who.int/news/item/11-12-2025-statement-gacvs-vaccines-autism)

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. What Happened in the Danish Cohort?

@body

Researchers followed 657,461 children using national registries. They could compare children who received MMR with children who had not received it.

- The adjusted hazard ratio for autism was 0.93. A ratio of 1 would mean equal rates over follow-up.
- The 95% confidence interval was 0.85 to 1.02. The data gave no evidence of increased risk.

@citation

[Hviid et al., MMR vaccination and autism (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831578/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Read the Confidence Interval](assets/set7_drawings/mmr_interval.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 5: Read the Confidence Interval\
The dot is the estimate. The interval crosses 1, the value for equal rates.

@citation

[Hviid et al., MMR vaccination and autism (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831578/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. Why Compare Children of Similar Ages?

@body

Imagine reading only reports from families who noticed developmental changes after a vaccination.

- That list has no count of children who had similar changes without a recent vaccination.
- The Danish study accounted for age and other differences between the groups. A timeline for one child cannot supply that comparison.

@citation

[The Lancet, Retraction of the 1998 case series (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20137807/)  |  [Hviid et al., MMR vaccination and autism (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831578/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 5. Two Different Records

@left

*1998 case series*

Twelve children. No comparison group. The paper was retracted in 2010.

@right

*2019 cohort study*

657,461 children. Registry data and a comparison group. The estimate did not show increased autism risk after MMR.

@citation

[The Lancet, Retraction of the 1998 case series (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20137807/)  |  [Hviid et al., MMR vaccination and autism (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831578/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. A Retraction Does Not Measure Risk

@body

- A retraction corrects the publication record.
- A cohort study estimates whether two groups had different rates over follow-up.
- These are connected questions, but they need different evidence.

@citation

[The Lancet, Retraction of the 1998 case series (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20137807/)  |  [Hviid et al., MMR vaccination and autism (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30831578/)

=== layout: subsection

# 6. Antibiotic Overuse

=== layout: bookend

# 6. Questions

@body

- How do uses of antibiotics in human and veterinary medicine compare?
- How do U.S. and European rules differ? Which uses should be restricted?
- How does antibiotic use on farms select for resistance?
- Compare two chicken companies' label claims. What do the claims cover, and why might consumers care?
- How can farms prevent infections and still treat sick animals? Who oversees those decisions?

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Selection Changes What Survives

@body

| After an antibiotic is present | What happens? |
| --- | --- |
| Susceptible bacteria | Many stop growing or die. |
| Resistant bacteria | More are able to survive and reproduce. |
| Population afterward | A greater proportion can resist that drug. |

@citation

[CDC, About antimicrobial resistance](https://www.cdc.gov/antimicrobial-resistance/about/index.html)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Which Cells Left Descendants?](assets/set7_drawings/bacterial_selection.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 6: Who Survives Exposure?\
In this example, resistance rises from 2 of 8 cells to 2 of 3 survivors.

@citation

[CDC, About antimicrobial resistance](https://www.cdc.gov/antimicrobial-resistance/about/index.html)

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Read the Scope of the Label

@body

- A claim about medically important antibiotics covers a specific subset of drugs.
- Ask whether the claim covers all antibiotics, the animal's entire life, and treatment of illness.
- Better infection prevention can reduce the need to treat animals.

@citation

[FDA, Judicious use of medically important antimicrobials](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/cvm-gfi-209-judicious-use-medically-important-antimicrobial-drugs-food-producing-animals)  |  [USDA FSIS, Substantiating animal-raising claims (2024)](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2024-09-10/pdf/2024-19696.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. A Policy Can Change the Reason for Use

@body

FDA's 2017 changes removed production uses, such as faster growth, from affected medically important antibiotics in feed and water.

- The change did not mean that sick animals should go untreated.
- A veterinarian still decides whether treatment is medically needed.

@citation

[FDA, 2017 antimicrobial sales report](https://www.fda.gov/media/119332/download)  |  [FDA, Judicious use of medically important antimicrobials](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/cvm-gfi-209-judicious-use-medically-important-antimicrobial-drugs-food-producing-animals)

=== layout: two-panels

# 6. Count the Right Outcome

@left

*Drug use*

How many kilograms of a drug were sold or used per animal?

@right

*Resistance*

What fraction of sampled bacteria resist a named drug, in a named setting?

@citation

[CDC, About antimicrobial resistance](https://www.cdc.gov/antimicrobial-resistance/about/index.html)  |  [FDA, 2023 summary report on antimicrobials sold or distributed](https://www.fda.gov/media/183070/download)

=== layout: two-panels

# 6. A Sick Animal and a Healthy Herd

@left

*Treatment*

A veterinarian treats an animal with a bacterial infection. The animal stands to benefit.

@right

*Growth promotion*

A producer wants healthy animals to gain weight. FDA calls for medically important antibiotics to serve animal health needs under veterinary oversight.

@citation

[FDA, Judicious use of medically important antimicrobials](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/cvm-gfi-209-judicious-use-medically-important-antimicrobial-drugs-food-producing-animals)

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Which Number Answers the Farm's Question?

@body

Imagine a farm buys half as much antibiotic this year, and its herd is half as large.

- Total purchases fell. Drug purchased per animal stayed the same.
- Neither number tells you how many bacterial samples resisted the drug. That takes a separate measurement.

@citation

[CDC, About antimicrobial resistance](https://www.cdc.gov/antimicrobial-resistance/about/index.html)  |  [FDA, Judicious use of medically important antimicrobials](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/cvm-gfi-209-judicious-use-medically-important-antimicrobial-drugs-food-producing-animals)

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. How Would You Check a Chicken Label?

@body

"No antibiotics ever" and "raised without medically important antibiotics" make different promises.

- A claim can cover all antibiotics or only a named class. Look for the exact words and the records behind them.
- Ask how the farm treats a sick bird and where that bird goes afterward. Animal welfare and label eligibility are separate questions.

@citation

[FDA, Judicious use of medically important antimicrobials](https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/cvm-gfi-209-judicious-use-medically-important-antimicrobial-drugs-food-producing-animals)  |  [USDA FSIS, Substantiating animal-raising claims (2024)](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2024-09-10/pdf/2024-19696.pdf)

=== layout: two-panels

# 6. Which Farm Uses Changed?

@left

*European Union, 2006*

The EU removed antibiotic growth promoters from its feed-additive register. Treating a sick animal is a separate use.

@right

*United States, 2017*

Production claims such as faster growth were removed from affected feed and water products containing antibiotics important in human medicine.

@citation

[EU Regulation 1831/2003, Article 11](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/PDF/?uri=CELEX%3A02003R1831-20151230)  |  [FDA, 2017 antimicrobial sales report](https://www.fda.gov/media/119332/download)

=== layout: subsection

# 7. Transgenic Plants

=== layout: bookend

# 7. Questions

@body

- What is an heirloom plant? What is a hybrid plant? Is it acceptable to develop plant hybrids using genetics? How do transgenic plants impact biodiversity and local ecosystems? How does public perception differ between transgenic plants and traditional hybrids? Can you discuss the role of transgenic plants in addressing food security challenges?

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Three Different Descriptions of a Crop

@body

| Term | What it tells you |
| --- | --- |
| Heirloom | The variety was maintained over generations. |
| Hybrid | Offspring came from a cross between genetically different parents. |
| Transgenic | The plant carries DNA introduced through genetic engineering from a different species. |

@citation

[University of Minnesota Extension, Saving vegetable seeds](https://extension.umn.edu/garden-and-home/yard-and-garden/gardening-minnesota/saving-vegetable-seeds)  |  [NHGRI, Transgenic](https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Transgenic)

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Start with the Trait

@body

- A breeder can select for sweetness over many generations.
- A transgenic crop can carry an introduced trait such as insect resistance.
- Ask which trait changed, what the comparison crop is, and what environmental effects were measured.

@citation

[FDA, GMO crops and their uses](https://www.fda.gov/food/agricultural-biotechnology/gmo-crops-animal-food-and-beyond)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Adoption of herbicide-tolerant soybeans](assets/set7_environment_evidence/ge_soybean_adoption.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 7: Which soybeans did U.S. farmers plant?\
Follow the share of planted acres. When did adoption start to level off?

@citation

[USDA ERS, adoption of genetically engineered crops, 1996-2024](https://ers.usda.gov/sites/default/files/_laserfiche/Charts/58020/biotechcrops2024_d.html?v=56818)

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Read the Adoption Trend Carefully

@body

- In 1996, 7.4% of U.S. soybean acres used herbicide-tolerant seed. In 2001, it was 68%.
- By 2007, adoption reached 91% and then stayed near that level through 2024.
- This series reports planted acres. A claim about yield, herbicide use, biodiversity, or farm profit needs another measurement.

@citation

[USDA ERS, adoption of genetically engineered crops, 1996-2024](https://ers.usda.gov/sites/default/files/_laserfiche/Charts/58020/biotechcrops2024_d.html?v=56818)

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Can the Same Seed Have Two Labels?

@body

Imagine a family has saved seed from the same tomato variety for four generations. A breeder crosses it with a different line.

- "Heirloom" tells you about the history of a parent.
- "Hybrid" describes the offspring of the cross. That word alone tells you nothing about whether either parent carried an introduced gene.

@citation

[University of Minnesota Extension, Saving vegetable seeds](https://extension.umn.edu/garden-and-home/yard-and-garden/gardening-minnesota/saving-vegetable-seeds)  |  [NHGRI, Transgenic](https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Transgenic)

=== layout: two-panels

# 7. Which papaya would you plant?

@left

![A papaya leaf showing a mosaic pattern associated with ringspot disease](assets/set7_case_photos/papaya_ringspot.webp)

@right

Look for the patchwork of pale and dark green on this leaf. The photograph shows symptoms of papaya ringspot.

In Hawaii, researchers grew resistant and susceptible trees. Besides counting sick trees, what would a grower want them to weigh?

@citation

[Photo: Plant pests and diseases, CC0](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papaya_%28Carica_papaya%29-_Papaya_ringspot.jpg)  |  [Ferreira et al., Hawaii field trials (2002)](https://doi.org/10.1094/PDIS.2002.86.2.101)

=== layout: two-panels

# 7. A Field Trial Needs Two Results

@left

*Disease outcome*

How many trees became infected with ringspot virus?

@right

*Farm outcome*

How much marketable fruit did each treatment produce?

@citation

[Ferreira et al., Field trials of virus-resistant papaya (2002)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30823304/)

=== layout: subsection

# 8. Dutch Elm Disease

=== layout: bookend

# 8. Questions

@body

- What is Dutch elm disease? What could Gary Strobel's unfinished trial establish about a possible treatment? What did Strobel do wrong during his research? How does Dutch elm disease compare to the emerald ash borer? What is being done to stop the ash borer?
- What were the ecological and economic impacts of Dutch elm disease?
- Can you compare the strategies used to combat Dutch elm disease and the emerald ash borer?
- What ethical considerations arise from using genetic engineering to combat tree diseases?

=== layout: one-panel

# 8. Two Tree Losses, Two Causes

@body

- Dutch elm disease is a fungal disease. Elm bark beetles can carry the fungus between trees.
- The fungus damages the tree's ability to move water.
- Emerald ash borer is an insect. Its larvae damage tissue under the bark of ash trees.

@citation

[American Phytopathological Society, Dutch elm disease](https://www.apsnet.org/edcenter/pdlessons/Pages/DutchElm.aspx)  |  [USDA APHIS, Emerald ash borer](https://www.aphis.usda.gov/plant-pests-diseases/eab)

=== layout: two-panels

# 8. A Thinning Canopy Is a Clue

@left

*Elm question*

Does the tree have evidence of a vascular fungal disease?

@right

*Ash question*

Does the bark reveal the insect's galleries or exit holes?

@citation

[American Phytopathological Society, Dutch elm disease](https://www.apsnet.org/edcenter/pdlessons/Pages/DutchElm.aspx)  |  [USDA APHIS, Emerald ash borer](https://www.aphis.usda.gov/plant-pests-diseases/eab)

=== layout: two-panels

# 8. Why an Outdoor Trial Needs Approval

@left

- In 1987, Gary Strobel began an unauthorized outdoor trial to control Dutch elm disease.
- The trial ended when the experimental trees were destroyed.
- A trial stopped early leaves the intended benefit uncertain. Who has a say before an outdoor release?

@right

![Montana State University portrait of plant pathologist Gary Strobel](assets/set7_people_early/gary_strobel.jpg)

@citation

[GAO, review of the Montana trial](https://www.gao.gov/assets/rced-88-64br.pdf)  |  [Portrait: Montana State University Library](https://arc.lib.montana.edu/msu-photos/item.php?id=2023)

=== layout: one-panel

# 8. Strobel's Trial and Independent Review

@body

- Why did the trial end before it could establish whether the treatment worked?
- An outdoor experiment can expose others to consequences beyond the experimenter's site.
- List the control group, the intended benefit, and one question the reviewer should ask.

@citation

[GAO, Role of institutional biosafety committees (1988)](https://www.gao.gov/assets/rced-88-64br.pdf)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Elm Canopy Decline](assets/set7_instructor/dutch_elm_disease.jpg)

@caption

Topic 8: Elm Canopy Decline\
Compare the canopy on both sides. What evidence would identify the cause of decline?

@citation

Course image: instructor ethics deck, p. 58.

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Emerald Ash Borers](assets/set7_instructor/emerald_ash_borer.jpg)

@caption

Topic 8: Emerald Ash Borers\
Two adult beetles on a coin. The larvae damage tissue under ash bark.

@citation

Course image: instructor ethics deck, p. 60.

=== layout: two-panels

# 8. Why did Strobel cut down the elms?

@left

![Gary Strobel holds a chain saw beside an elm as students watch, 1987](assets/set7_people_early/strobel_elm_1987.jpg)

@right

Gary Strobel is about to cut down one of his experimental elms. Montana State students watched in 1987.

The EPA had yet to approve the trial. What could the researchers still learn after the trees were gone?

@citation

[Photo: Montana State University Library (1987)](https://arc.lib.montana.edu/msu-photos/item.php?id=2822)  |  [GAO, trial review](https://www.gao.gov/assets/rced-88-64br.pdf)

=== layout: two-panels

# 8. What Review Needs Before a Trial

@left

In 1958, Charles Webster sprayed elms for the village of Maple Bluff, Wisconsin. A village ordinance called for the work.

Decades later, Strobel wanted to test a new treatment in Montana. Who should decide where a trial can take place? What would make them stop it?

@right

![Charles Webster spraying elm trees in Maple Bluff, Wisconsin, 1958](assets/set7_people_early/elm_spraying_1958.jpg)

@citation

[Photo: Wisconsin Historical Society (1958)](https://www.wisconsinhistory.org/Records/Image/IM141318)  |  [GAO, review of the Montana trial](https://www.gao.gov/assets/rced-88-64br.pdf)

=== layout: two-panels

# 8. Start with the Host Tree

@left

*Elm*

Test for the fungus. Look for evidence of disrupted water transport.

@right

*Ash*

Look beneath the bark for feeding galleries left by larvae. A thin canopy alone fits either tree.

@citation

[American Phytopathological Society, Dutch elm disease](https://www.apsnet.org/edcenter/pdlessons/Pages/DutchElm.aspx)  |  [USDA APHIS, Emerald ash borer](https://www.aphis.usda.gov/plant-pests-diseases/eab)

=== layout: subsection

# 9. Genetic Invasive Species Control

=== layout: bookend

# 9. Questions

@body

- How do ecological models predict the impact of removing an invasive species?
- What are the social and political challenges in releasing engineered organisms into ecosystems?

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Removing the Invader Changes the Ecosystem

@body

- An invasive species can compete with native species or become prey for other animals.
- A model can test how outcomes change if these relationships are uncertain.
- Compare removing the invader, leaving it in place, and continuing existing control.

@citation

[Jones, Seabird island recovery after rat removal (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21265441/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Who Should Get a Voice?

@body

- An intervention can affect land and communities beyond the original site.
- Ask who monitors benefits and harms, who reviews the evidence, and who can stop a project.
- Consultation matters before a decision is made.

@citation

[National Academies, Gene Drives on the Horizon (2016)](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/resource/23405/Gene-Drives-Brief.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. After the Rats Are Gone

@body

On seabird islands, rats prey on birds. Nesting birds also bring nutrients from the sea onto land.

- The researcher compared soil and plant indicators across islands with different rat histories.
- A count of the remaining rats answers one question. Recovery of the island's food web can take much longer.

@citation

[Jones, Seabird island recovery after rat removal (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21265441/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 9. Two Clocks After Control

@left

*Immediate*

Did the local control remove or reduce the invasive species?

@right

*Longer term*

Did native species, soil, and plant indicators change as predicted?

@citation

[Jones, Seabird island recovery after rat removal (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21265441/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Compare Islands Before Claiming Recovery

@body

An island without rats can differ from an island with rats for many reasons.

- A strong comparison uses several islands and records the rat history of each one.
- Soil and plant measurements help test whether a food web changed after removal.

@citation

[Jones, Seabird island recovery after rat removal (2010)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21265441/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Where Should Monitoring Stop?

@body

A hypothetical island project monitors a seabird colony on the release site. Fishing families on the neighboring coast ask to take part.

- Their concern widens the map of who may bear costs.
- Agree on a baseline, who will receive the reports, and what observation would trigger a new decision.

@citation

[National Academies, Gene Drives on the Horizon (2016)](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/resource/23405/Gene-Drives-Brief.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 9. Rats leave. What should you measure next?

@body

- Rat eradication can remove a predator without restoring an island overnight.
- On New Zealand islands, seabird-derived nutrients in soil, plants, and spiders recovered over decades after rat removal.
- A project should decide whether its next measurement is rats, breeding birds, or the ecosystem that birds help support.

@citation

[Jones, Seabird island recovery after rat removal (2010)](https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1890/10-0118.1)  |  [National Academies, Gene Drives on the Horizon (2016)](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/resource/23405/Gene-Drives-Brief.pdf)

=== layout: subsection

# 10. Bioweapons

=== layout: bookend

# 10. Questions

@body

- What international agreements, such as the Biological Weapons Convention, restrict the development of bioweapons?
- What responsibilities do scientists have when research could be misused?

=== layout: two-panels

# 10. The Biological Weapons Convention

@left

President Richard Nixon and Secretary of State William Rogers attended the Washington signing on April 10, 1972.

The treaty bans biological weapons and protects peaceful work in biology. How could countries share information about an unusual outbreak?

@right

![President Nixon and Secretary of State William Rogers at the Biological Weapons Convention signing, Washington, April 10, 1972](assets/set7_people_early/bwc_signing_1972.jpg)

@citation

[UN treaty, Arts. I and X](https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%201015/volume-1015-I-14860-English.pdf)  |  [WHO (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)  |  [Photo: Nixon Library, NARA](https://2021-2025.state.gov/celebrating-fifty-years-of-biological-weapons-nonproliferation/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 10. A Review Committee's Questions

@body

- A research team claims its work will benefit public health.
- What public health outcome would count as a benefit? Could a lower-risk approach answer the same question?
- Who should participate in independent review?

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: one-panel

# 10. Read Two Articles Together

@body

Article I commits parties to keep biological agents and toxins only in types and quantities justified by protective, preventive or other peaceful purposes. It also prohibits biological weapons.
Article X addresses cooperation for peaceful uses of biology. Vaccine and public health work belong in that conversation.

@citation

[United Nations, Biological Weapons Convention, Articles I and X](https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%201015/volume-1015-I-14860-English.pdf)

=== layout: two-panels

# 10. Treaty Language and a Lab Decision

@left

*Treaty question*

Is the work limited to protective, preventive, or other peaceful purposes?

@right

*Institution question*

Does the project have a clear public-health use and independent oversight?

@citation

[United Nations, Biological Weapons Convention, Articles I and X](https://treaties.un.org/doc/Publication/UNTS/Volume%201015/volume-1015-I-14860-English.pdf)  |  [WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: one-panel

# 10. Who Receives the Concern?

@body

A trainee worries that a project's risks were overlooked. The project leader is also the trainee's supervisor.

- An institution can provide a confidential route to an independent reviewer.
- The reviewer needs enough information to assess the concern and a way to tell the trainee it was heard.

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: one-panel

# 10. What Can a Government Report Show?

@body

Under the Biological Weapons Convention, states exchange annual confidence-building information. The UN maintains a reporting portal.

- A public report can make stated activities visible and invite follow-up questions.
- A missing report leaves uncertainty. It cannot by itself establish what happened inside a country.

@citation

[UN Office for Disarmament Affairs, Confidence-building reports](https://bwc-cbm.un.org/reports)

=== layout: one-panel

# 10. A Missing Report Has a Limited Meaning

@body

- A submitted report makes a country's stated information available for questions and comparison.
- A missing report leaves a gap in the public record.
- Neither result directly measures work that was never reported.

@citation

[UN Office for Disarmament Affairs, Confidence-building reports](https://bwc-cbm.un.org/reports)

=== layout: subsection

# 11. Gain-of-Function and Supervirus Research

=== layout: bookend

# 11. Questions

@body

- How do scientists decide whether a virus-modifying experiment is too risky to perform?
- How do scientists weigh the benefits of gain-of-function research against the possibility of accidental release?

=== layout: one-panel

# 11. A Broad Term and a Specific Concern

@body

- Gain of function describes a change that gives a biological system a new or increased activity.
- The public debate about pathogen research concerns risks to people outside the lab as well as risks to workers.
- A review asks whether the expected knowledge justifies the risk.

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: one-panel

# 11. Compare Evidence, Alternatives, and Oversight

@body

- Identify the public health question in plain English.
- Ask whether existing samples, observational data, or computation can answer it.
- Document remaining uncertainties and who is responsible for oversight.

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Research review starts with the decision](assets/set7_environment_evidence/review_decision.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 11: Who could use the result?\
Name a decision a health department could make, then work back to the evidence.

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: one-panel

# 11. What Decision Is the Committee Making?

@body

A public health team proposes a study. The review panel first asks what decision a health department could make with the result.

- A clear answer lets the panel weigh the likely benefit.
- If existing observations already answer the question, further work may add risk without adding much knowledge.

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: one-panel

# 11. Who Can Reopen the Review?

@body

Suppose a project's expected public benefit changes halfway through the funding period.

- The original benefit-risk judgment used information that has changed.
- Give the review group a defined opportunity to pause, reconsider alternatives, and record its reasons.

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)

=== layout: two-panels

# 11. What Would Change the Decision?

@left

*Benefit changes*

New evidence shows that the result would no longer guide a public-health decision.

@right

*Risk changes*

New information changes who could be affected or whether the safeguards still fit.

@citation

[WHO, Responsible use of the life sciences (2022)](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240056107)
